├── pipeline/
│   ├── transform.py
│   ├── loader.py
│   └── sources/
│       ├── db_source.py
│       └── email_source.py
├── scripts/
│   ├── create_tables.py
│   ├── seed_client_db.py
│   └── make_excel_email.py
├── sql/
│   └── create_tables.sql
├── sample_data/
│   └── email_inbox/
└── powerbi/
    └── siagrow_dashboard.pbix
```

 How to Run

Start ClickHouse:
```
docker compose up -d
```

Install the requirements:
```
python -m venv .venv
.venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

Create the table in ClickHouse:
```
python scripts/create_tables.py
```

Generate sample data:
```
python scripts/seed_client_db.py
python scripts/make_excel_email.py
```

Run the pipeline once:
```
python run_pipeline.py
```

Or run it automatically every minute:
```
python scheduler.py
```

Then open `powerbi/siagrow_dashboard.pbix` and click Refresh.

 The ClickHouse Table

All sources end up in one table, `siagrow.orders`, with the same columns:

| Column | Description |
|---|---|
| record_id | unique id for each order |
| source | where the order came from (database or email) |
| source_id | the original order id |
| customer_name | customer name |
| product | product name |
| quantity | quantity ordered |
| unit_price | price per unit |
| total_amount | quantity × unit price |
| city | city |
| order_date | order date |
| received_at | when the data was received |
| ingested_at | when it was loaded into ClickHouse |

Power BI reads from the view `siagrow.orders_clean`, which returns the data without duplicates.

 What I Would Add Next

- Reading real emails from a mailbox using IMAP.
- Adding WhatsApp as another source using a FastAPI webhook.
- Using Airflow for scheduling and monitoring.